# Written before the Dockerfile on purpose. Everything below either must not
# reach the build daemon or must not survive into an image layer.

# Licensed textbooks, government circulars and working screenshots. .gitignore
# records that these are "never published with the source", and the directory is
# 1.9 GB. It is absent from a CI checkout because git already ignores it, so the
# case this guards is a `docker build` run from someone's own machine, where the
# whole 1.9 GB would otherwise be handed to the daemon and could land in a layer
# that gets pushed to a registry.
km/

# Real credentials. A secret copied into a layer stays in that layer even if a
# later step deletes the file.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Rebuilt inside the image. Copying a host build in would mix a Windows-built
# tree into a Linux image.
node_modules/
.next/
out/
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Unrelated design reference, per .gitignore.
skill/

# Agent and editor state the application never reads.
.git/
.github/
.claude/
.agents/
.agent/
.design/
*.log
.DS_Store

# The one large file under docs/. Nothing reads it, and it is 3.5 MB of the
# 5.1 MB that directory weighs.
#
# The rest of docs/ stays, and that is deliberate: src/server/project-status.ts
# reads docs/roadmap/roadmap.json and docs/handoff/ at request time, through a
# path built from process.cwd(). Next's tracer cannot see a path assembled at
# runtime, so those files are not pulled into the standalone output on their own
# and the Dockerfile has to copy them in. Excluding docs/ here would make
# /roadmap and /api/project-status throw in production while passing every test.
docs/SLA-License-Apple.pdf

# drizzle/ is NOT excluded. `drizzle-kit migrate` needs both the .sql files and
# drizzle/meta/_journal.json to know which migrations have already run.
